<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Polish People's Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1947 CE – 1989 CE · capital Warsaw</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Communist one-party state of 1947 to 1989 under Soviet influence, brought down by the Solidarity movement and the Roundtable Talks of 1989.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Polish People's Republic grew out of the Provisional Government of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, set up on 1 January 1945 by the Polish Workers' Party after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took Polish territory from German occupation. The communists consolidated control through the 1946 referendum and the January 1947 election, both tainted by fraud. The Small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-japan">Constitution of 1947</a> named the state the Republic of Poland, and the Constitution of 1952 gave it the name People's Republic.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Polish United Workers' Party (PZPR), formed in 1948, held a monopoly of power in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> in which the Ministry of Public Security and later the Security Service acted as secret police. Industry was nationalised, and German territory east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-neisse-line">Oder–Neisse line</a> was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Bolesław Bierut led the state until his death in March 1956; Władysław Gomułka then eased the Stalinist system, and Edward Gierek replaced him after price rises caused protests in December 1970. Heavy borrowing from the West under Gierek left a debt that brought a decade of economic crisis. The political scientist R. J. Rummel estimated that at least 22,000 people were killed by the regime.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The trade union <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a>, founded in September 1980 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-walesa">Lech Wałęsa</a>, had nine million members by the end of 1981, and General Wojciech Jaruzelski proclaimed martial law on 13 December 1981. Roundtable Talks in 1989 led to a June election that Solidarity won, and Tadeusz Mazowiecki became prime minister. The constitution was amended on 29 December 1989 to remove the party's guaranteed power, beginning the Third Polish Republic of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/poland">Poland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/polish-peoples-republic.jpg" alt="Polish People's Republic" width="330" height="207"><figcaption>Image: Arvedui89 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Poland_(1928–1980).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1947 CE – 1989 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Warsaw</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bolesław Bierut, Władysław Gomułka, Edward Gierek, Wojciech Jaruzelski</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Poland (1947–1952), PRL</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1968&amp;focus=polish-peoples-republic" aria-label="Polish People's Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Polish People's Republic lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4531 5631L4531 5631M4531 5631L4531 5631"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4531 5631h0M4531 5631h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4531 5631h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4531 5631h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Warsaw. <a href="/atlas?year=1968&amp;focus=polish-peoples-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
